<!--
    kuna: AArch64 sign-extending byte/halfword loads into a W register.

    A W destination sign-extends to 32 bits and zeroes bits 63:32 (Arm ARM,
    LDRSB/LDRSH/LDURSB/LDURSH/LDTRSB/LDTRSH, 32-bit variant).  Upstream's
    AARCH64base.sinc sign-extended ldrsb/ldrsh/ldursh/ldtrsh W into all 64
    bits of X (`ldrsh w0,[x0]` of 0xd564 gave x0 = 0xffffffffffffd564, the
    processor 0xffffd564) and zero-extended ldursb/ldtrsb W.  Found by
    comparing one-instruction lifts with Unicorn.

    Hand assembled (clang -target aarch64-linux-gnu), one W form per
    constructor, the result read as 64 bits:
      lsh       ldrsh  w0,[x0,#4]          (unsigned offset)
      lsb       ldrsb  w0,[x0,#1]
      lsh_idx   ldrsh  w0,[x0,x1,lsl #1]   (register offset)
      lsb_idx   ldrsb  w0,[x0,x1]
      lsh_neg   ldursh w0,[x0,#-2]
      lsb_neg   ldursb w0,[x0,#-1]         (was a zero extension)
      lsh_pre   ldrsh  w8,[x0,#2]!; add x0,x0,x8
      lsb_post  ldrsb  w8,[x0],#1;  add x0,x0,x8
      tbl       ldrsh  w8,[x0]; ldr x0,[x1,x8,lsl #3]
    Controls, X destinations (unchanged):
      lsh64     ldrsh  x0,[x0]
      lsb_neg64 ldursb x0,[x0,#-1]

    Strict SLEIGH fix, no option.  Before: `return (int8)p[2];`,
    `return (uint1)p[-1];`, `return t[*p];`.  After: `(uint4)p[2]`,
    `(uint4)p[-1]`, `t[(uint4)*p]`.
-->
